# Ground truth for jumpingjack_10_mohit_paced.csv
#
# First jumping-jack recording, and the first trace in the library for an exercise other
# than the squat. Performed in time with a metronome, which is a precondition and not a
# detail: JumpingJackRepDetector pairs two impacts per rep, and that 2.0 bursts-per-rep
# ratio only holds at a paced cadence. See the pacing note in that class.
#
# Channel: TotalAcceleration.csv from the Sensor Logger export, the same channel the
# Android squat traces use, so gravity is included and the thresholds have a resting
# ~9.81 to sit against.

exercise = JUMPING_JACK

# 10 jacks inside the window below. The recording runs ~28.7 s in total; the set is the
# middle stretch, with the phone being pocketed before it and retrieved after.
reps = 10
setStartMs = 8000
setEndMs = 21000
tolerance = 0
fullTraceEvents = 10

# 10 over the whole recording -- the same as the in-set count, which is NOT what the squat
# traces do. Every squat trace reports 12 across the raw file: 10 reps plus two
# phone-handling bursts that clear every guard.
#
# Pairing is what removes them here. A handling burst is a lone event, and a lone event
# never finds a harder partner to pair with, so it is held as a candidate jump-out and then
# displaced by the next burst. Requiring two ordered impacts turns out to reject
# single-burst artifacts for free, which the one-burst-per-rep squat model cannot do.
#
# No quiet window is declared: no still stretch has been identified in this recording.

notes = participant: Mohit. Pixel, ~99Hz, front pocket, metronome-paced. First jumping-jack trace; the detector's thresholds (minBurstAmplitude 4.1, minBurstDurationMs 130) were proposed from burst statistics, and this recording is the first real test of them.
